    11	#   five regions: top / left / anchor / right / bottom, exactly like a coarse tiling around the yellow center.
    12	# - D2 contributes the local interface geometry: every non-anchor parent -> child edge is rendered as a slot/tab pair.
    13	#   Concretely, the parent owns a 2-cell red overlap segment on the shared boundary and also has a 2-cell cavity one
    14	#   step inside its body; the child overlaps the same two red cells and extends a same-color tongue into that cavity.
    15	# - Small trace on one vertical piece-piece interface:
    16	#   original cbebaa4b:
    17	#     parent and child only share the two red cells; after pairing, the surrounding silhouette can stay jagged.
    18	#   fused variant:
    19	#     parent has red-red boundary cells plus two black cells directly behind them; child has the same red pair plus a
    20	#     two-cell tongue entering those black cells; after placement, the tongue fills the hole and the union still has to
    21	#     complete the surrounding rectangle.
    22	#   difference:
    23	#     a candidate move is not judged just by "reds can overlap". Its geometry must also avoid solid-solid collisions,
    24	#     fill visible cavities, and still support a globally solid rectangular final silhouette.
    25	#
    26	# b) Complexity calibration
    27	# - The rectangle is always partitioned into five visually interpretable regions, so humans can infer the macro-goal
    28	#   from a few examples instead of learning many unrelated layout classes.
    29	# - To keep the cavity behavior visible and repeated, we only sample spanning trees whose anchor degree is exactly 2.
    30	#   That guarantees exactly two plain anchor links and exactly two piece-piece slot/tab links in every instance.
    31	# - Region thicknesses come from small integer ranges (4-6), large enough to make enclosed holes obvious while keeping
    32	#   the overall rectangle comfortably within the 22-26 canvas.
    33	#
    34	# c) Hidden assumptions audited from the original main()
    35	# - Original `_try_place()` allowed same-color body overlap. That becomes unsafe once solid slabs and tongues exist, so
    36	#   the fused solver allows overlap only on red-red cells.
    37	# - Original completion accepted any all-paired red assembly. That no longer matches the task: completion must also be
    38	#   a solid rectangle, and every interior cavity extracted from the input pieces must be covered in the final board.
    39	# - Original extraction ignored interior black cells inside piece bounding boxes. Here those holes are semantic, so the
    40	#   solver records them and uses them both for move scoring and for final validation.
    41	# - Original generator assumed a free-form connection tree could be rendered independently per piece. Here the global
    42	#   rectangle and the local slot/tab geometry must be designed together, so generation is output-first from a rectangle
    43	#   ownership grid.
    44	#
    45	# d) Placement legality
    46	# - Each region starts as a disjoint solid block inside the target rectangle.
    47	# - Plain anchor links reuse the D14 trick: parent and child both own the same two red boundary cells.
    48	# - Slot/tab links remain legal because the parent deletes the two cavity cells, while the child alone owns those two
    49	#   cells as a protrusion; therefore the only overlaps are still exactly the two red cells.
    50	# - After the assembled rectangle is shifted into the canvas, only non-anchor pieces are scattered with padding.
    51	#
    52	# e) Visual observability
    53	# - Across examples, a human sees two simultaneous regularities:
    54	#   1. the output is always one filled rectangle centered on the yellow region;
    55	#   2. some scattered non-anchor pieces visibly contain enclosed black slits that disappear only when a neighboring
    56	#      piece slides in with a matching tongue.
    57	# - No invisible ordering uses numeric color ids. Colors are persistent sprite identities only.
    58	#
    59	# f) Visual cleanliness
    60	# - The assembled output is a single clean rectangle.
    61	# - Cavities are always short 2-cell slits inside thick rectangular pieces, so they are legible instead of noisy.
    62	# - Overlap depth stays simple: only red-red overlap is ever permitted.
    63	#
    64	# g) Inference burden
    65	# - There is no color remapping rule. The challenge is purely geometric: infer local slot/tab compatibility and the
    66	#   global rectangular objective from repeated visual evidence.
    67	#
    68	# h) Generator-side anti-degeneration intent
    69	# - Every generated sample contains exactly two slot/tab interfaces, hence at least four interior hole cells in the
    70	#   scattered input. That guarantees the D2 behavior is not accidentally absent.
    71	# - The assembled board is explicitly checked to be a solid rectangle, so the D14 behavior is always active.
    72	#
    73	# i) New solver assumptions guaranteed by the generator
    74	# - Exactly one piece contains yellow (4): enforced by the five-region construction.
    75	# - Every valid overlap is red-red only: enforced when interfaces are carved.
    76	# - All interior holes come from deliberate slot geometry and are fillable by the matching child tongue.
    77	# - Exactly one assembly satisfies the fused constraints: `generate_input()` verifies this by running `main()`.
